<section class="section wrap" id="human-data">
 <div class="section-head"><div class="section-title-with-prop"><HandshakeArt variant="accent" object="data" /><p class="label">12 / HUMAN DATA · 2026</p><h2>Human data is evolving.<br/>Expert judgment matters.</h2></div><p>Three developments I’m following in 2026: ongoing expert curation, more efficient use of human annotations, and closer attention to why annotators disagree.</p></div>
 <div class="human-data-thesis"><p class="label">2026 HUMAN DATA DEVELOPMENTS / MY READING</p><p>These 2026 examples show different ways human expertise shapes data quality: maintaining difficult evaluation sets, assessing synthetic data and interpreting disagreement. I read them as practical signals for data operations, rather than a measure of the whole market.</p>
 <div class="human-trend-grid">
  <article><span class="label">01 / EXPERT CURATION · SEPT 2026</span><h3>Expert data needs ongoing upkeep.</h3><p>The September 17 update to HLE-Rolling describes internal checks, outside-expert feedback and replacement of easier questions. Expert-created evaluation data also needs correction and renewal as models improve.</p><p><strong>My operational takeaway:</strong> track disputed examples, record revisions and keep evaluation versions traceable.</p><a href="https://labs.scale.com/leaderboard/humanitys_last_exam" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Scale / CAIS · September 17, 2026 update ↗</a></article>
  <article><span class="label">02 / DATA EFFICIENCY · IJCAI 2026</span><h3>Use human annotations deliberately.</h3><p>SynCABEL studies synthetic training examples for biomedical entity linking. In its tested settings, it matched full human supervision with up to 60% less annotated data. That is a task-specific result, not evidence that expert labeling is unnecessary everywhere.</p><p><strong>My operational takeaway:</strong> compare synthetic data against trusted evaluation cases before changing the human-review workload.</p><a href="https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2026/763" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IJCAI 2026 · SynCABEL ↗</a></article>
  <article><span class="label">03 / ANNOTATOR DISAGREEMENT · JULY 2026</span><h3>Disagreement can carry information.</h3><p>An ACL 2026 paper models group-level variation in subjective annotations. Its synthetic judgments aligned only moderately with human annotations, and the best mixing strategy depended on the dataset.</p><p><strong>My operational takeaway:</strong> investigate whether disagreement comes from an error, unclear guidance or a legitimate difference in perspective before resolving it.</p><a href="https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.1914/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ACL · July 2026 research ↗</a></article>
 </div>
 <p class="human-trend-connection"><span class="label">MY TAKEAWAY FOR HANDSHAKE</span>I see a connection to contributor matching, clear annotation instructions and consistent review. In the Safety SPA role, I would help identify gaps, investigate disagreements and turn quality findings into guidance the team can use.</p>
 </div>
</section>
